<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionian Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionian Sea is the part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and Albania, linked to the Adriatic in the north by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-otranto">Strait of Otranto</a>. The Ionian Islands, including Corfu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a>, lie along its eastern side. In antiquity it carried the main sea routes between Greece and the Greek colonies of Italy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionian-sea.jpg" alt="Ionian Sea" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_23.91172E_39.08554N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dyme lay on the coastal plain of western Achaea, near Cape Araxos, facing the <b>Ionian Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dyme">Dyme</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing Zancle across the Strait, it controlled shipping between the Tyrrhenian and <b>Ionian Seas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rhegion">Rhegion</a></li></ul></section>
